section \<open>Computational Path Presentations\<close>

theory CP_Introduction
  imports Main
begin

text \<open>This entry formalizes the algebraic, endpoint-typed core of
computational-path presentations. Finite binary witnesses are flattened to
composable signed words and reified as typed canonical trees. An independent
typed tree congruence, generated by groupoid equations and context rules, is
related to free word equivalence by soundness and completeness proofs. The
correspondence also extends to named tree equations, translated only when both
original trees have the declared endpoints. This guard prevents flattening from
activating an otherwise ill-typed declaration. Given executable equality on generator labels, a cancellation algorithm
computes the free normal form and refines the normal form in Breitner's AFP entry
Free Groups \<^cite>\<open>Breitner2010FreeGroups\<close>. Its soundness and completeness are
for the free structural word congruence, defined independently by cancellation
steps, symmetry and transitivity.

Typed equations can then be declared between parallel words. Interpretation
by finite words preserves these equations under explicit typing and
relation-preservation hypotheses. Adjoining a fresh primitive together with
an equation defining it as an existing word is conservative on the old
presentation: eliminating that primitive reflects equality between old words.
The original equation set may be arbitrary; this does not assert a decision
procedure for equality in an arbitrary presentation. Relation replacement and
redundant-equation theorems justify changes of defining equations. Reversible
word interpretations give preservation and reflection from explicit primitive
roundtrip hypotheses, and transport complete invariants between presentations.

A separate one-object presentation has two loop generators and the single
equation AB = BA. Its signed exponent pair and canonical signed-power word
give a complete normal form for this particular nonempty presentation, both
for words and for independently generated binary-tree equations. The
result is algebraic: no identification with the geometric torus is asserted.
Isabelle/HOL already supplies general free-abelian-group algebra; this result
concerns the exact independently generated word congruence and its explicit
normalization, not a new abstract classification theorem.

The motivating literature treats equality witnesses as explicit computational
paths \<^cite>\<open>Ramos2017Identity\<close> and \<^cite>\<open>deVeras2021Topological\<close>.
The book \<^cite>\<open>Ramos2026Calculus\<close> motivates the presentation
semantics, independent tree equations and torus normal form. Manuscript versions
were inspected; their section numbers and pagination are distinguished from
the final published edition in the source-correspondence guide. The
presentation-change viewpoint and the distinction between literal signed
words and binary syntax follow the algebraic setup of
\<^cite>\<open>Ramos2026Presentation\<close>. The present scope is deliberately narrower
than its topological results: no quotient topology, realization homeomorphism,
geometric completeness or equality of binary and equal-slot parametrizations
is claimed. The cancellation relation is not the full LND equality rewrite
calculus. Executable endpoint checking additionally requires executable
equality on the vertex type. The abstract HOL correctness statements are
independent of these code-execution requirements.

The earlier dependent-HIT development already has typed walks and presented
path quotients. This entry uses the existing AFP free-group cancellation core
and adds an executable refinement, independent tree adequacy, explicit
presentation changes and a specific nonempty-presentation decision procedure. It has no import-based connection to an intensional identity type.
A future HoTT interpretation must prove its endpoint and equation contracts
explicitly. The independent tree congruence concerns eight structural groupoid
equations; its equational completeness does not prove termination or confluence
of a full oriented LND equality rewrite calculus.\<close>

end
